          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "4.3.2 Dry cells"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.107,
            0.745,
            0.144
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Voltaic cells were an important discovery, but they are not convenient for everyday use. Instead, dry cells are one of the most"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.144,
            0.573,
            0.292
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "widely used electric cells today. They are called ‘dry’ because the electrolyte is not a liquid but a thick moist paste. The structure of a dry cell is shown in Fig. 4.9. It consists of a zinc container which acts as a negative terminal and a carbon rod at the centre covered with metal cap that acts as the positive terminal. The carbon rod is surrounded by the paste-like electrolyte."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.293,
            0.573,
            0.37
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The dry cell is a single use cell, meaning once it is used up, it has to be disposed of. For several applications, rechargeable batteries are increasingly being used now."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.579,
            0.148,
